WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:10.814
Babylon, over 2,600 years ago. Along the river, King Nabopolassar's workmen were building a wall of baked brick, to keep its water out.

00:00:12.514 --> 00:00:18.169
A wall like that was never done: king after king repaired it, on the same line, for centuries.

00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:24.830
Once a brick was laid among millions, nobody could tell who had made it, or what it was for.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:32.288
And a king feared his work would be forgotten, or his words removed by a king who came after him.

00:00:34.088 --> 00:00:37.689
Every stretch of wall needed to say who had built it, and what for.

00:00:39.689 --> 00:00:46.134
So on the edges of his baked bricks, his workmen stamped or wrote the king's name, and the work the brick was for.

00:00:47.834 --> 00:00:56.209
One reads: "Nabopolassar, king of Babylon, … the one who built the embankment of the Arahtu River for the god Marduk, his lord."

00:00:58.409 --> 00:01:06.086
About two and a half thousand years later, archaeologists dug out the river wall, and its bricks still said which king built each stretch.

00:01:07.786 --> 00:01:12.803
In one stretch alone, they mapped sixteen of Nabopolassar's bricks, still in place.

00:01:14.803 --> 00:01:19.797
At the utility today, every element on the map says who owns it and where it came from.

00:01:21.197 --> 00:01:26.011
So every change can be checked, and walked through what it touches, before anyone builds.

00:01:33.611 --> 00:01:40.732
Tomás's map changes every day now: 140 changes last month, each confirmed by its owner.

00:01:42.532 --> 00:01:49.772
Planned work is when the network's crews switch a street off, to mend or replace its lines. Households get a letter first.

00:01:51.572 --> 00:01:56.341
Last year, planned work moved to a new table, and the letters kept reading the old one.

00:01:58.141 --> 00:02:04.852
For three weeks, 1,900 households got the wrong date. On the real day, their power went off without warning.

00:02:06.752 --> 00:02:10.977
Nobody had done anything wrong. Nobody could see what read the old table.

00:02:12.777 --> 00:02:19.048
Now the head of retail asks, in a ticket, for a text to each household two days before planned work.

00:02:20.748 --> 00:02:25.607
Before anyone builds it, Tomás needs to know what it touches, and who must agree.

00:02:29.007 --> 00:02:37.096
Like a stamped brick, every element says who owns it and where it came from. So the map can be tested, as code is, on every change.

00:02:38.796 --> 00:02:45.255
Every element has an owner and a source. Every capability is realised by a process or a system.

00:02:47.055 --> 00:02:53.579
Every data object stands for a business object. No element is left unlinked, and none is there twice.

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:58.444
And every rule has a home, and a case that must fail.

00:03:00.244 --> 00:03:07.476
A check that has never failed may be checking nothing. So each rule's check is shown a change that breaks it, and must stop it.

00:03:09.576 --> 00:03:14.778
Sending the network's list of homes with solar panels to retail must fail. It does.

00:03:16.578 --> 00:03:22.510
In software, checks like these are called fitness functions: tests that the architecture still holds.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:28.749
A change that fails one waits, until it's fixed, or its owner says why.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:39.261
Walking a change through every layer took Tomás a day, for the social tariff. The map agent can draft a walk in minutes.

00:03:40.961 --> 00:03:46.044
But before anyone relies on its walks, it's tested on a change whose answer is known.

00:03:47.744 --> 00:03:52.017
The social tariff touched fourteen elements, and five owners had to agree.

00:03:53.817 --> 00:04:00.569
The first time, the agent finds thirteen. It misses the secure exchange that carries the agency's answers.

00:04:02.469 --> 00:04:08.545
On the map, nothing linked the agreement to it. Tomás knew it from his walk, but nobody had drawn the line.

00:04:10.345 --> 00:04:17.697
The gap was the map's. So the line is drawn, and a check added: every agreement with a partner says how its data travels.

00:04:19.497 --> 00:04:27.625
Run again, it finds all fourteen, and the five owners. The test runs again whenever its model or its instructions change.

00:04:30.925 --> 00:04:35.753
Now the new change. The agent walks it down the map, from the ticket to the tables.

00:04:37.453 --> 00:04:41.604
Planned work is the network's: Grace's teams plan it, street by street.

00:04:43.204 --> 00:04:47.401
Phone numbers are retail's: each retailer holds its own customers'.

00:04:49.001 --> 00:04:57.451
So the network tells every retailer, on the same terms, which homes lose power and when, and each retailer texts its own customers.

00:04:59.251 --> 00:05:05.779
Down to the tables: the planned work, the supply points it cuts off, and each retailer's customer contacts.

00:05:07.579 --> 00:05:14.649
One element is new: a texting service, with no owner yet. The checks stop there, until the head of retail takes it.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:23.444
It lists who must agree: Grace, the head of retail, Ama for the rule at the wall, and Inés for the data between them.

00:05:25.344 --> 00:05:28.913
Each with its ID and its evidence, so a person can check it.

00:05:32.013 --> 00:05:37.549
The map and the code say how things are meant to run. Only the running estate shows how they do.

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:45.230
So the walk is checked against the estate itself: what reads each table, and what runs each night.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:52.613
The agent reads there as it reads the map: read-only, with personal and sensitive data masked.

00:05:54.213 --> 00:06:00.497
It finds a reader the map doesn't show: the contact centre's software keeps its own copy of phone numbers.

00:06:02.297 --> 00:06:08.207
When a household calls with a new number, the copy changes. The customer system never hears of it.

00:06:10.007 --> 00:06:15.796
The data team counts the difference: one number in eleven. The texts would have gone to old numbers.

00:06:17.896 --> 00:06:24.970
The head of retail decides: the customer system is the source, and the contact centre's changes go back to it first.

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:29.323
And the copy goes on the map, with an owner.

00:06:32.423 --> 00:06:37.306
In Babylon, the river wall said, brick by brick, who had built each stretch, and what for.

00:06:39.006 --> 00:06:46.186
When Nabopolassar found an older king's inscription in a wall, in his own words, he did not change its location.

00:06:47.786 --> 00:06:51.239
And he asked the kings after him to keep his words with theirs.

00:06:52.839 --> 00:07:00.291
At the utility, every element carries its owner and its source, so every change is checked, and walked, before anyone builds.

00:07:01.691 --> 00:07:08.588
In April, the first texts go out: 3,400 households, each two days before the crews reach its street.

00:07:10.188 --> 00:07:12.291
Every one has the date the crews have.

00:07:17.991 --> 00:07:24.148
Give every element an owner and a source, and test the map on every change, with a case that must fail.

00:07:25.548 --> 00:07:31.617
Walk each change through all it touches, check it against what's really running, and let the owners decide.

00:07:33.517 --> 00:07:34.893
Next: kept alive.
